#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bottle-stores: games from other stores (GOG, Epic Games) in Steam's library.

Steam in the bottle can't run a store's own launcher, so Portalistic does what Heroic does: it
downloads a game with the store's download tool (GOG: gogdl, Epic: Legendary, both vendored in
the kit), puts it in one of the game folders, and adds it to Steam as a "non-Steam game" that
runs through Proton Experimental like every Steam game (bottle-steamplay sets that, bottle-dxvk
adds DXVK 2.6.2). The game then shows in Steam's library and in Big Picture, under the
collection "GOG" or "Epic Games".

  bottle-stores gog status [--json]       signed in? how many games installed
  bottle-stores gog login-url             the address to sign in at (in a browser)
  bottle-stores gog login CODE|ADDRESS    finish signing in with what the browser ended on
  bottle-stores gog logout                forget the sign-in (installed games stay)
  bottle-stores gog list [--json] [--refresh]
                                          the games the account owns that have a Windows version
  bottle-stores gog folders [--json]      where games can go (the Mac's game folders, the VM's disk)
  bottle-stores gog install ID [--folder NAME] [--lang CODE]
                                          download a game (or bring an installed one up to date)
  bottle-stores gog remove ID [--purge]   delete a game's files and its Steam entry (--purge: also
                                          its Wine prefix, which holds saves and settings)
  bottle-stores epic status [--json]      the same for Epic Games:
  bottle-stores epic login-url            the address to sign in at
  bottle-stores epic login CODE|TEXT      finish signing in with what Epic's page shows
  bottle-stores epic logout
  bottle-stores epic list [--json] [--refresh]
  bottle-stores epic folders [--json]
  bottle-stores epic install ID [--folder NAME]
  bottle-stores epic remove ID [--purge]
  bottle-stores sync [--force]            write the installed games into Steam's shortcuts (Steam
                                          must not be running: steam-arm64 runs this before it
                                          starts Steam, which keeps its lists in memory)
  bottle-stores ids                       the Steam app IDs of those shortcuts (bottle-steamplay)
  bottle-stores status                    every installed game
  bottle-stores run -- COMMAND...         run COMMAND (a game) with Comet beside it, see below
  bottle-stores run --epic ID -- COMMAND...
                                          run COMMAND (an Epic game) with Epic's sign-in added

Games that use GOG's Galaxy SDK (a Galaxy.dll or Galaxy64.dll in their folder) ask the Galaxy
client's background service for the player's sign-in, achievements and leaderboards; without it
they say "User is not signed in. To continue, start the GOG Galaxy client". The VM can't run that
client, so the kit carries Comet (https://github.com/imLinguin/comet, Apache-2.0), an open-source
stand-in for that service, which Heroic uses too. For such a game the Steam shortcut's launch
options are "bottle-stores run -- %command%": `run` signs Comet in with the GOG sign-in already
held here (through a private link to gog-auth.json; no token goes on a command line), starts it
on port 9977 unless it already runs, waits for the Galaxy files it fetches from GOG on first use
(about 27 MB, kept in ~/.local/share/comet), runs the game, and stops the Comet it started when
the game ends. Without Comet, or without a sign-in, `run` just runs the game.

Epic's games start differently: the game itself asks for a sign-in token on its command line
(-AUTH_PASSWORD=...), and a token works once, for a few minutes. So an Epic game's launch options
are "bottle-stores run --epic ID -- %command%": `run` asks Legendary for the arguments (it
signs in with the saved login and gets a fresh token) and starts the game with them. If that
doesn't work the game still starts, without the sign-in. The kit does nothing about anti-cheat
(Easy Anti-Cheat, BattlEye): games that need it may not start.

Files, in ~/.config/steamos-bottle/stores:
  gog-auth.json     gogdl's sign-in (a secret: mode 600; never printed)
  gog-user.json     the account's name; gog-library.json the games list (a cache)
  legendary/        Legendary's own folder: user.json (Epic's sign-in, a secret: mode 600),
                    installed.json, metadata/ ...; epic-library.json is the games list (a cache)
  games.json        installed games: {"gog": {ID: {title, dir, exe, args, startdir, appid, galaxy, ...}},
                    "epic": {APPNAME: {...}}}
  art/              artwork downloaded for them
  comet-cfg/        a link to gog-auth.json where Comet looks for it (it reads Heroic's file name)
Comet's own log: ~/.cache/steamos-bottle/comet.log; the Epic launches': epic.log next to it.
Output lines starting with "@@" are for the Mac app (download progress).
"""
